data.content:
## .NET 8 Migration: `TimedExecutor`

### Key Breaking Changes

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | **Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** |
| Thread-based timeout | Use `CancellationToken` + `Task` |

### Why `Thread.Abort()` Was Removed

`Thread.Abort()` injected `ThreadAbortException` at arbitrary points, corrupting state — partially written fields, unreleased locks, broken invariants. It was never safe. .NET Core/5+ removed it entirely.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);
            return task.GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Async overload (preferred)
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token).WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Usage

```csharp
var executor = new TimedExecutor();

// Sync — work must check the token
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(ct =>
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative check
        // ... do work ...
    }
    return 42;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

// Async (preferred)
var data = await executor.ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync(async ct =>
{
    return await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url, ct);
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));
```

### Critical Pitfalls

**1. Cancellation is cooperative, not preemptive.** If `work` never checks the token, it runs forever. Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, nothing forcibly kills it.

```csharp
// BAD — uninterruptible, will never time out
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(ct =>
{
    Thread.Sleep(TimeSpan.FromHours(1)); // ignores token
    return 42;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

// GOOD — use cancellable APIs
executor.ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync(async ct =>
{
    await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromHours(1), ct); // respects token
    return 42;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

**2. `Task.Run` uses the ThreadPool.** The old code created a dedicated thread. If `work` is long-running, use `Task.Factory.StartNew` with `TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning`.

**3. `WaitAsync` (used above) was added in .NET 6.** It lets the caller stop waiting even if the inner task hasn't completed — essential for true timeout behavior when calling code you don't control.

**4. Signature change is breaking.** Callers must update from `Func<T>` to `Func<CancellationToken, T>`. If you need a compatibility shim:

```csharp
// Wrapper for legacy callers — but timeout won't interrupt the work
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    return ExecuteWithTimeout(ct => work(), timeout);
}
```

**5. Exception wrapping changed.** The original threw `AggregateException`; the migrated version propagates directly via `GetAwaiter().GetResult()`. If callers catch `AggregateException`, update them.
